1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,767
Hi — I'm Divij Pawar. Two agents look at the exact same

2
00:00:02,767 --> 00:00:05,933
evidence — same filing, same numbers, same question — and come back

3
00:00:05,933 --> 00:00:06,867
with opposite answers.

4
00:00:07,233 --> 00:00:11,000
Agent A is confident the company's margins are improving. Agent B is

5
00:00:11,000 --> 00:00:14,433
just as confident they're not. Both wrote clean, well-reasoned explanations.

6
00:00:14,833 --> 00:00:17,867
Both cited real sources. Neither one is lying — they just can't

7
00:00:17,867 --> 00:00:21,500
both be right. Today: what actually happens the second two agents disagree,

8
00:00:22,267 --> 00:00:25,467
and why the instinctive fix — average their answers and move on

9
00:00:25,467 --> 00:00:27,233
— is about the worst thing you could do.

10
00:00:27,930 --> 00:00:30,597
The obvious move is to blend the outputs — average the two

11
00:00:30,597 --> 00:00:33,097
confidence scores, split the difference on the conclusion,

12
00:00:33,463 --> 00:00:35,430
ship something moderate and hedged, and move on.

13
00:00:35,797 --> 00:00:37,197
That instinct is exactly wrong.

14
00:00:37,663 --> 00:00:40,297
Averaging two agents who disagree doesn't get you closer to the truth

15
00:00:40,363 --> 00:00:42,563
— it gets you an answer that looks moderate but is really

16
00:00:42,563 --> 00:00:43,797
nobody's actual opinion.

17
00:00:44,430 --> 00:00:47,130
If Agent A is right and Agent B is wrong, blending them

18
00:00:47,130 --> 00:00:48,563
just makes the true answer worse.

19
00:00:49,197 --> 00:00:51,730
And it throws away the one piece of information in the whole

20
00:00:51,730 --> 00:00:53,697
exchange that was actually valuable:

21
00:00:54,097 --> 00:00:55,730
the fact that they disagreed at all.

22
00:00:56,197 --> 00:01:00,530
A controller that quietly smooths conflicting outputs into a neutral-sounding grade

23
00:01:00,530 --> 00:01:04,497
has taken the most informative moment in the pipeline and erased it.

24
00:01:05,110 --> 00:01:08,843
So: treat disagreement between independent agents as a signal, not noise, and

25
00:01:08,843 --> 00:01:12,443
don't blend it away. Detect it on structure — confidence and direction

26
00:01:12,510 --> 00:01:13,310
— not wording.

27
00:01:13,477 --> 00:01:16,477
Bound the debate to a single round. And if it's still unresolved,

28
00:01:16,643 --> 00:01:20,177
it has to reach the final output looking unresolved, not smoothed into

29
00:01:20,177 --> 00:01:20,977
one grade.

30
00:01:21,010 --> 00:01:23,243
It lowers your risk. It doesn't take it to zero.

31
00:01:23,930 --> 00:01:26,330
Your turn. Look at a system you're building or using with more

32
00:01:26,330 --> 00:01:28,863
than one agent — even two steps that each produce a judgment.

33
00:01:29,463 --> 00:01:32,797
First, name the structured signal you'd actually compare if they disagreed: a

34
00:01:32,797 --> 00:01:35,097
confidence score, a directional call, not the raw text.

35
00:01:35,597 --> 00:01:38,030
Second, pick a threshold — how big a gap actually counts as

36
00:01:38,030 --> 00:01:40,363
a real disagreement, versus just different phrasing.

37
00:01:40,963 --> 00:01:44,597
Third, decide, before it ever happens, what your system does with 'unresolved'

38
00:01:44,597 --> 00:01:45,397
— where it shows up,

39
00:01:45,963 --> 00:01:46,997
and who sees it first.

